Subject: [PATCH] media: rockchip: cif: allow variable-length CSI-2 frames

The rkcif driver assumes every CSI-2 frame carries exactly
width * height * bpp bytes.  When a frame is shorter (which is the
normal case for a JPEG byte stream tunneled over CSI-2), two things
go wrong:

  1. CSI_SIZE_ERR fires.  Its ISR branch increments csi_size_err_cnt,
     sets RKCIF_ERR_SIZE and then *returns*, so the FRAME_END handling
     for the same interrupt is skipped and the buffer is never queued.
  2. That same ISR branch writes CIF_REG_MIPI_LVDS_CTRL |= 0x000A0000,
     which soft-resets the CSI host, tearing down the stream.

There is a second guard right after it:

  if (intstat & CSI_ALL_ERROR_INTEN_V1) { all_err_cnt++; return; }

and CSI_SIZE_ERR is a subset of CSI_ALL_ERROR_INTEN_V1
(CSI_SIZE_ERR = bits 24..27, CSI_ALL_ERROR_INTEN_V1 = 0xf0f << 16),
so masking only the first branch would not be enough.

FRAME_END is triggered by the CSI-2 FE short packet, not by the byte
count, so it still arrives for a short frame.  Masking CSI_SIZE_ERR out
of the INTEN write therefore lets variable-length frames complete
normally, while every other error interrupt stays enabled.

Payload bytes beyond the actual transfer length are left as whatever
the buffer held before (stale frame data).  Consumers are expected to
locate the real payload themselves (e.g. scan for FF D8 .. FF D9).

Target: RK3576, kernel 6.1.99, SDK mlk-sdk-3576-v1.1.0.

---
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/cif/capture.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/cif/capture.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/cif/capture.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/cif/capture.c
@@ -4615,8 +4615,15 @@
                        rkcif_write_register(dev, CIF_REG_MIPI_LVDS_CTRL, val);
                }
 
+               /* Anlogic: allow variable-length CSI-2 frames (JPEG byte stream).
+                * CSI_SIZE_ERR not only drops the frame, its ISR branch also
+                * soft-resets the CSI host, so a payload shorter than
+                * width*height can never complete.  FRAME_END is a packet,
+                * so it still fires without a size match.  Keep every other
+                * error interrupt enabled.
+                */
                rkcif_write_register_or(dev, CIF_REG_MIPI_LVDS_INTEN,
-                                       CSI_ALL_ERROR_INTEN_V1);
+                                       CSI_ALL_ERROR_INTEN_V1 & ~CSI_SIZE_ERR);
        }
 #if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_CPU_RV1106)
        if (channel->id == 1)
